  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM MDT Sun Jun 7 2026\n\nAn UL ridge is building across NM this afternoon in between a\ntrough over the western quarter of the CONUS and a trough over the\nCentral Plains. This ridge will expand while drifting east into\nthe Southern Plains. Temperatures will climb as a result with most\nlowland locations in the upper 90s and lower 100s.\n\nThe long wave trough to our west will nudge eastward, aligning\nitself to our north by late Tuesday and early Wednesday. A weak\ns/w trough will cut out ahead of it Tuesday afternoon, which will\nbe enough to bring low storm chances to the Sacramento Mountains.\nBy Wednesday, with the trough aligned to our north, breezes will \npick up for areas west of the Rio Grande. The deep westerly flow \nwill also push temperatures to the hottest point in the forecast \n(for now). Heat Advisories are a good bet with highs pushing 105, \nespecially across the Texas Rio Grande Valley, including El Paso. \n\nThe subtropical ridge will re-build from the south to finish out\nthe period. Both the GFS and Euro agree fairly well with the\nstrength and placement of the high by next weekend, but differ in\nhow much moisture can slip in beneath the ridge. The amount of\nmoisture will have implications on whether our string of 100\ndegree days continue with further warming or if we will see some\nmodest relief i.e. mid to upper 90s along with a chance for\nshowers and thunderstorms. The NBM has spotty low POPs, and that\nis what is in the forecast.",
